5. Completion criteria
The rebuild should not be considered complete simply because npm run build passes.
It is complete when all of these are true:
•	 A branch administrator can create multiple independent configurations.
•	 Each configuration controls its own contribution amount, frequency, capacity and collection positions.
•	 Tenants are created and sequenced independently under each configuration.
•	 Members are allocated dynamically without duplicate positions.
•	 Tenants activate only when full and all members are verified.
•	 Contribution and payout schedules start from actual activation.
•	 Contributions work at capacities above 12.
•	 Every configured payout position is processed correctly.
•	 TVC and the 1.5% fee are calculated correctly.
•	 Tenure completion and renewal work dynamically.
•	 Database constraints, API validation and user-facing messages contain no fixed 12-member assumptions.
•	 Automated tests verify the complete lifecycle at different capacities.
•	 The rebuilt backend compiles, starts, migrates and operates successfully.
Implementation order: database → configuration management → tenant provisioning → activation and scheduling → contributions → payouts and TVC → renewal → supporting modules → tests → deployment.
This is the rebuild scope. It does not include changing the frontend at this stage. The backend must first provide a complete, reliable dynamic-configuration API for the frontend to consume. No code changes were made as part of this analysis.

